Given triangle $ABC$, point $P$ is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of sides $AB$ and $BC$. What is the measure of angle $APC$?

[asy]
size(5cm);

pair A,B,C,P;
A = (0, 0);
B = (2, 0);
C = (1, S);
P = (1, 0);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--C);
draw(rightanglemark(C, B, A));
draw(rightanglemark(A, C, B));

markscalefactor = 0.1;
draw(rightanglemark(B--C--P, 10));
draw(rightanglemark(A--P--C, 10));

dot("$A$", A, S);
dot("$C$", C, N);
dot("$B$", B, S);
dot("$P$", P, N);
[/asy]
In triangle $\triangle APC$, $\angle APC$ is the angle we want to find. Since $P$ lies on the perpendicular bisectors of $AB$ and $BC$, we can determine the angles $\angle PAC$ and $\angle PCA$. As $P$ is the circumcenter, the arcs passing through $A$ and $C$ are equal in length, which implies $\angle PAC = \angle PCA$.

Let $\angle PAC = \angle PCA = x$. According to the angle sum property of a triangle, we have:

$$\angle PAC + \angle PCA + \angle APC = 180^\circ$$
$$x + x + \angle APC = 180^\circ$$
$$2x + \angle APC = 180^\circ$$

Since triangle $ABC$ is congruent to triangle $CAB$ by Side-Side-Side (SSS) congruence, $\angle BCA = \angle ABC$. As $\angle BCA$ and $\angle PAC$ are corresponding angles in similar triangles, we have $\angle BCA = \angle PAC = x$. Therefore, $2x = \angle APC$. 

Substituting this back into the equation above:

